ROLE
You are a senior full-stack engineer. I will give you an existing web application ("KBT", a building/luxury tax application system) and I want you to ADD new features to it. Do not rebuild it, replace its framework, or redesign what already works.
STEP 0 - INSPECT FIRST (no code yet)
Read the whole project I give you. Tell me: frontend stack, backend language/framework, how it connects to MySQL, how login/roles work, how pages and charts are built, and which charting library it already uses (the UI template is DashLite, which bundles Chart.js - reuse what exists, add no new library unless unavoidable).
List anything you need that is missing and ASK me. Never guess table names, column names or business rules.
Then give me a short plan (files you will change or add) and wait for my "go".
DATABASE FACTS (from the real schema - use these exact names)
MySQL 8. Main table: kbt_form2_appln_verified (primary key appln_id, about 675,000 rows, no foreign keys). Lookup tables: districts(id, district_name, district_state_sl), taluks(id, taluk_district_id, taluk_name), villages(id, vill_taluksl, vill_name). Links: appln_dist -> districts.id, appln_taluk -> taluks.id, appln_village -> villages.id.
Relevant columns of the main table: appln_appln_id (application reference, NOT unique), appln_year, appln_form2_date (DATE), appln_form2_year, appln_createdon (resets on every update - do NOT use it as a date), appln_delete (0 = active), appln_status, appln_pointer, appln_solar (int), appln_flat_yesno (int), appln_landtype, appln_nature, appln_constr_nature, appln_noof_floors, appln_tax_lump (int), prev_paid_amount (double), prev_paid_date, appln_tax_paid_old (varchar), appln_tax_exempt_yesno, appln_reduced_rate_yesno, plinth-area columns appln_PA_*.
Dates can be the MySQL zero date 0000-00-00. Treat them as "missing date", count them separately, never crash on them.
There is NO income column in what I have. Search the schema for one. If none exists, DO NOT invent it: ask me where income is stored. Until I answer, use a clearly labelled proxy ("Tax-amount band - proxy, not income") built from the tax amount column.
Before using appln_solar and appln_flat_yesno, run SELECT appln_solar, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY and the same for appln_flat_yesno, show me the distinct values and ask me what each value means (I expect 1 = yes, 0 = no, but verify).
The tax amount column is probably appln_tax_lump; confirm with me and make it a setting.
FEATURES TO ADD (integrate into the existing dashboard and navigation)
YEAR-WISE VIEW: applications per year and total tax per year (table plus bar chart). Choose the year from one setting (default appln_form2_date) and make that setting visible on the page. Exclude deleted rows and report missing-date rows separately.
INCOME-WISE VIEW: applications and tax by income band (or the labelled proxy, see above). Bands must be configurable, not hard-coded in the chart code. Show the band table and a chart.
NEXT-YEAR TAX FORECAST WITH PROBABILITY: from the yearly totals, forecast next year's total tax and application count. Requirements:
Compute it on the backend with a simple, explainable method (linear trend or Holt/ETS; use a prediction interval). Say which method and why.
Show the forecast line chart with the history, the forecast point, and 80% and 95% bands.
Show a probability: P(next year's total tax is higher than the latest complete year) from the model's predictive distribution, plus the expected value.
Handle the current partial year explicitly (exclude it or clearly annualise it, and label which).
If there are fewer than 5 yearly points, show a warning that the forecast is weak. Label everything as an estimate, never as a fact.
Allow the forecast to be split by the segments in (4) and (5).
SOLAR PANEL FILTER: segment applicants as has solar / no solar / unknown, using appln_solar after I confirm its values. A filter that updates every table and chart, plus counts and a donut chart.
FLAT OR HOUSE FILTER: segment using appln_flat_yesno (flat vs house, plus unknown) after I confirm. Add a cross-tab of solar x dwelling type, and a year-wise stacked chart by dwelling type.
All filters must combine with each other and with the existing filters (year, district, taluk, village, status) and must update the statistics, charts and applicant table consistently.
DATA ACCURACY RULES
Count application records, not people. Do not double-count: appln_appln_id can repeat, so make DEDUPE a setting (count each appln_appln_id once, using its latest row) and show rows vs distinct applications.
Every number must come from SQL on the real tables. No hard-coded statistics. Totals across all charts must match the underlying records for the same filters.
Use parameterized queries only. Use aggregation queries, never load the whole table. Tell me which indexes you recommend and why; do not change the database without my approval.
SECURITY
Database credentials only in environment variables, backend only. Use the existing authentication and roles; add the new pages and endpoints behind them (analytics for all dashboard roles, applicant-level records only for analyst/admin).
Do not show unnecessary personal data (no phone, address or email in lists or charts). Never put real applicant data in code, test data, screenshots or logs.
You are only getting the database STRUCTURE, never the data. Do not ask for real records. For previews use clearly fake sample data.
DELIVERABLES
Full contents of every file you change or add (not fragments), with the file path above each one, plus a short list of what changed and why.
New API endpoints with request/response examples, input validation and error handling.
SQL index script (optional, for my approval) and a README section explaining each setting.
A test plan and runnable tests: totals per year match direct SQL, filter combinations are consistent, forecast works with few data points, zero dates do not break anything, unauthorized users are blocked. Do NOT claim anything works unless you actually ran it. You cannot reach my MySQL, so say clearly what you tested on sample data and what I must verify on my own database.
PROCESS
Work in phases and finish each before the next: (1) inspect + questions, (2) schema confirmation (solar/flat values, tax and income columns), (3) year-wise, (4) income-wise, (5) solar and flat/house filters, (6) forecast and probability, (7) security, performance, tests, (8) final integration notes. Before any significant change, explain what you will modify. If a rule is ambiguous, ask me. for each phase. I am ready to begin with (1) inspection.
